<Base title={title} description={description} path="/guides/hex-rgb-and-hsl-color-codes-explained.html">
<aside class="ad-rail" aria-label="Advertisement"><div class="ad-slot ad-slot-rail"></div></aside>
    <div class="wrap article">
      <p class="note"><a href="/guides.html">Guides</a> / Color</p>
      <h1>Colour Codes Explained: HEX, RGB and HSL</h1>
      <p class="lead">Every colour on a screen can be written as a code. The three common ways are HEX, RGB and HSL. They describe the same colours in different ways, so pick whichever is easiest for your task.</p>
    <div class="ad-slot" aria-label="Advertisement"></div>
<h2>HEX: a short code</h2>
<p>A HEX code, such as #5B5BD6, is a single code for a colour. Each pair of letters and numbers gives the amount of red, green and blue, from 00 (none) to FF (the most). Designers copy these codes from their design tools.</p>
<h2>RGB: three amounts</h2>
<p>RGB describes the same colour as three numbers from 0 to 255: how much red, how much green and how much blue. For example, rgb(91, 91, 214) is the same colour as #5B5BD6.</p>
<h2>HSL: how a colour looks</h2>
<p>HSL describes a colour by its hue (the colour itself, as a position on a colour wheel), how strong it is, and how light or dark it is. It is handy when you want a lighter or darker version of the same colour.</p>
<h2>Which one to use</h2>
<ul>
<li>Use HEX when you copy a colour from a design program.</li>
<li>Use RGB when you need a see-through colour.</li>
<li>Use HSL when you want to make a colour lighter or darker.</li>
</ul>
      <section class="worked">
        <h2>Worked example</h2>
        <p>The colour #5B5BD6 is three pairs. 5B is 91, 5B is 91 again and D6 is 214. The same colour written in the other style is rgb(91, 91, 214).</p>
      </section>
      <section class="faq-list">
        <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
        <details><summary>What does #FFF mean?</summary><p>It is white. Each short symbol is repeated, so FFF is the same as FFFFFF.</p></details>
        <details><summary>Which colour style should I use on my website?</summary><p>Any of them works. Use whichever your design program gives you.</p></details>
        <details><summary>When is HSL useful?</summary><p>It is easiest when you want a lighter or darker shade of a colour you already have.</p></details>
      </section>
